{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining right-skewness as a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes. In this decision problem, both lotteries have a 66%–67% chance of yielding 0 Euros (unfavorable outcome) and a 33%–34% chance of yielding a positive amount (favorable outcome). Thus, both lotteries are right-skewed, but the degree of skewness can be assessed by comparing the distribution of probabilities. Lottery A has a slightly higher probability of the favorable outcome (34% vs. 33%) and a slightly lower probability of the unfavorable outcome (66% vs. 67%). Therefore, Lottery A is less right-skewed than Lottery B. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which would be Lottery B. The relevant information for the reason is thus available and can be derived from the probabilities and outcomes.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'Die Risiko-Unterschiede wirken auf mich marginal. Jedoch will ich anmerken, dass ich bei Wahlmöglichkeit weder auf A noch auf B eingegangen wäre.' This translates to: 'The differences in risk affect me only marginally. However, I would like to note that I would not have chosen either A or B.' The individual acknowledges that risk differences are marginal and explicitly states that they did not choose between A or B. There is no mention of evaluating skewness, probability distributions, or any reasoning related to right-skewness. The deliberation process described is minimal and focuses on the marginal nature of risk differences, without any reference to skewness or the structure of the probability distributions in terms of favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes. Thus, no justification based on skewness is provided.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason—specifically, the comparison of skewness—is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention or evaluate the distribution of probabilities in terms of unfavorable vs. favorable outcomes. Instead, they describe risk differences as marginal and state they would not have chosen either option. Since the individual neither identifies nor applies the rule about preferring the more right-skewed lottery, the decision reason was not used. The justification provided does not align with the rule of the decision reason. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason to make the decision.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones. In this decision problem, Lottery A has an 80% chance of 4000 Euros and a 20% chance of 0 Euros. This means the bulk of the probability (80%) is on a favorable outcome (4000 Euros), and only 20% is on an unfavorable outcome (0 Euros). Thus, Lottery A is left-skewed (more weight on favorable outcomes). Lottery B has 100% chance of 3000 Euros — a certain outcome with no risk or unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, Lottery B is not right-skewed at all; it is symmetric (or left-skewed, depending on interpretation), with no unfavorable outcomes. Since no lottery is right-skewed (i.e., greater weight on unfavorable outcomes), the decision reason would prefer a lottery that is more right-skewed — but neither A nor B meets that criterion. Thus, the decision reason cannot be applied meaningfully here because no lottery is right-skewed. The reason is not triggered by the structure of the lotteries.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'Bei einem pot. Verlustgeschäft bei nichteintreten meines Gewinns hätte ich hier nicht das Risiko gewählt, aber 80% sind recht gute Chancen.' This translates to: 'In a potential loss situation if my gain does not materialize, I would not have chosen the risk, but 80% are quite good chances.' The individual is expressing a preference for avoiding risk in the event of a loss (i.e., a risk-averse attitude), and acknowledges that 80% chance is favorable. However, there is no mention of skewness, unfavorable outcomes, or any evaluation of the distribution of probabilities in terms of left or right skew. The individual does not refer to the structure of outcomes in terms of unfavorable vs. favorable probabilities, nor does the individual compare the skewness of the lotteries. The justification centers on risk aversion and the perception of 80% as good, not on skewness or the distribution of outcomes in terms of unfavorable weight. Thus, the deliberation process described does not involve analyzing skewness or the proportion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness, specifically identifying a right-skewed lottery (where unfavorable outcomes have greater probability weight). In this case, neither lottery is right-skewed — Lottery A is left-skewed (80% favorable), and Lottery B is certain (no unfavorable outcomes). Therefore, the condition for applying the reason is not met. Moreover, the verbal report does not mention skewness, nor does it reference the probability distribution in terms of unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the individual justifies the choice based on risk aversion and the perception of 80% as a good chance. There is no evidence that the individual considered or applied the skewness-based rule. Since the reason is not applicable to the lotteries and not reflected in the justification, the individual did not use the decision reason. Thus, the assessment is that the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

